{"schema_version":1,"date":"2026-09-30","exercise":2,"folder":"2026-09-30_AppointmentReminders","title":"Appointment Reminders","topic":"Interfaces & dependency injection -- without overengineering","topic_index":2,"domain":"healthcare admin (clinic appointment reminders)","difficulty":2,"status":"struggled","key_learnings":["Define an interface by the narrow behavior the consumer needs (one Send method), not by the concrete type being replaced","The dependency is injected through the constructor; the service no longer builds *SMTPClient itself"],"mistakes":["Abstracted at the wrong layer: made the whole service an interface while the SMTPClient stayed hardwired inside SendReminder","Built for the SMS fallback (Breaking Point/Bonus) before solving the core seam, which overengineered it (Services/EmailService/PhoneService)","Returned a pointer to an interface and called a method on a type that did not have it, so the code did not compile"],"review_changes":["Introduced a one-method Mailer interface satisfied by *SMTPClient","NewReminderService takes a Mailer instead of host/port","Message building extracted into a pure function so it can be previewed and tested without a relay"],"next_focus":["Revisit interface-at-the-consumer with a small follow-up before moving on","Practice solving the core seam first and leaving extension requirements for later"]}
